        "thinking": "OK, there's already a `SidecarClient` class in `sidecar-client.ts`. However, it uses a `baseUrl + apiKey` interface, not a `SIDECAR_SERVICE` DO namespace. This is the higher-level typed client, while the lower-level routing (DO vs URL) is what needs to be shared.\n\nThe existing `SidecarClient` doesn't solve the DO vs URL routing problem from F-08. The three places (plugins.ts, heartbeat.ts, plugin-dispatch.ts) all have:\n\n\n1. If `SIDECAR_SERVICE`: use DO binding\n2. Else: use URL + apiKey\n\nI need to add a lower-level function to `sidecar-client.ts` that handles this routing. The DO namespace in the workflows comes from `this.env.SIDECAR_SERVICE` which is typed `DurableObjectNamespace`, while the SidecarClient currently just takes a URL. I should create a helper function that accepts either the DO binding or URL+apiKey and handles the routing internally.\n\nLooking at the three call sites, they're all making POST requests to different internal paths — heartbeat, plugin jobs, and plugin proxying — so a unified routing helper would eliminate duplication across heartbeat.ts, plugin-dispatch.ts, and plugins.ts.\n\nI'll add a lower-level helper function to sidecar-client.ts that handles the DO versus URL routing logic, accepting the service config, HTTP method, path, and body as parameters. I'm realizing the KV reads need different handling depending on context — in heartbeat.ts they're inside the retry step so moving them is fine, but in plugin-dispatch.ts they're pre-read outside the step to avoid retrying KV operations, so I'll pass them as parameters instead. Now I'm questioning whether extracting this into a shared module is worth the added complexity, since the three implementations have subtle differences in their error handling and service routing. The 503-vs-fallback distinction comes down to what the caller passes: `url: null` for plugins.ts (which should fail hard when there's no sidecar) versus `url: bridgeUrl` for workflows (which can fall back to localhost). Now I'm implementing this refactor.",
